      "Setbacks should be extracted as minimum separation distances.",
      "A setback is an enforceable minimum separation the facility must maintain from the target; do not extract a distance that merely triggers a disclosure, identification, mapping, notification, inventory, study, or applicability requirement (for example a requirement to identify any airport within one mile of the line), even when a distance and length unit are stated.",
      "A setback row's value must be a measured separation distance in a length unit; record it in the unit stated by the ordinance, preferring 'feet' or 'meters' when the text uses them. Omit the feature entirely only when no measured separation distance in a length unit is stated for that feature.",
      "Do not extract a number that is part of a named term or classification rather than a measured distance (for example the return period in a '100-year floodplain', a voltage tier, or a zoning-district code); such numbers are not setbacks.",
